Robot <-> computer protocol

One TCP connection, robot is the client. All multi-byte values are little-endian,
float is IEEE-754 32-bit.



ROBOT -> COMPUTER

Binary packet protocol:

[TYPE][PAYLOAD]

TYPE     uint8_t

where:
  TYPE     - packet type identifier
  PAYLOAD  - data interpreted according to TYPE

Current TYPEs:
  TYPE           CODE    TOTAL PACKET SIZE (including TYPE)

  RESPONSE       0x52    13 bytes
  STATE          0x53     5 bytes
  LIDAR          0x54    47 bytes
  VELOCITY       0x56    13 bytes
  ANGLE          0x41    13 bytes

Robot sends in a loop (about 20 Hz): responses, state, lidar, velocity, angle.
Packets follow each other with no separator.



RESPONSE binary packet protocol (after every finished or rejected request):

- Id:     4 bytes, uint32, id of the request
- Status: 4 bytes, uint32, 0 - succeeded, 1 - failed
- Time:   4 bytes, uint32, duration of the action in ms

TOTAL PAYLOAD SIZE: 12 bytes
TOTAL PACKET SIZE:  13 bytes including TYPE

STATE binary packet protocol (on every state change and every 10 s):

- State: 4 bytes, uint32, 0 - Idle, 1 - Calibrating, 2 - Ready, 3 - Processing, 4 - Failure

TOTAL PAYLOAD SIZE: 4 bytes
TOTAL PACKET SIZE:  5 bytes including TYPE

Response of a finished request is always sent before the state change it causes.

LIDAR binary packet protocol:

Raw bytes from LiDAR UART are forwarded without parsing, so one send can
contain several frames or end in the middle of a frame.

- Data Length: 1 byte (upper 3 bits reserved, lower 5 bits = number of points, fixed 12)
- Radar speed: 2 bytes (deg/s)
- Start angle: 2 bytes (0.01 degree)
- DataPoints: 3 bytes each point (distanceValue: 2 bytes mm, confidence: 1 byte)
- End angle: 2 bytes (0.01 degree)
- Timestamp: 2 bytes (ms, wraps at 30000)
- CRC8: 1 byte checksum

TOTAL PAYLOAD SIZE: 46 bytes
TOTAL PACKET SIZE:  47 bytes including TYPE

VELOCITY binary packet protocol:

- Velocity:  4 bytes, float, along direction of travel (m/s, negative - backward),
             Kalman filter of accelerometer and encoders
- Timestamp: 8 bytes, int64, time of the estimate (us since robot boot)

TOTAL PAYLOAD SIZE: 12 bytes
TOTAL PACKET SIZE:  13 bytes including TYPE

ANGLE binary packet protocol:

- Heading:   4 bytes, float, since robot boot (rad, clockwise positive, not wrapped to +-pi),
             Kalman filter of gyroscope and encoders
- Timestamp: 8 bytes, int64, time of the estimate (us since robot boot)

TOTAL PAYLOAD SIZE: 12 bytes
TOTAL PACKET SIZE:  13 bytes including TYPE

VELOCITY and ANGLE are sampled from a 50 Hz estimator, the same timestamp
in both packets means the same estimate.



COMPUTER -> ROBOT

Binary packet protocol, same as robot -> computer:

[TYPE][PAYLOAD]

Current TYPEs:
  TYPE           CODE    TOTAL PACKET SIZE (including TYPE)

  REQUEST        0x51    18 bytes

Robot skips bytes until it finds TYPE, so after a lost or extra byte
following requests are read correctly again.

REQUEST binary packet protocol:

- Type:           4 bytes, uint32, 0 - move, 1 - rotate,
                  2 - stop (reserved, not implemented yet, answered as failed)
- Distance:       4 bytes, float, distance for move (m, negative - backward)
- Rotation angle: 4 bytes, float, angle for rotate (degrees, clockwise positive)
- Id:             4 bytes, uint32, returned in RESPONSE
- Power mode:     1 byte, uint8, max PWM duty x/255, at least 100
                  (100 - ultraSlow, 150 - slow, 180 - normal, 200 - mediumFast, 220 - fast, 255 - overdrive)

TOTAL PAYLOAD SIZE: 17 bytes
TOTAL PACKET SIZE:  18 bytes including TYPE

Requests are queued (up to 8) and executed one at a time.
Request is answered as failed at once when the queue is full or robot is in Failure,
and after it is taken from the queue when its type is unknown or its power mode is lower than 100.
